<T>` is covariant over `T`. This is usually the correct"},{"DocComment":" choice for most data structures and safe abstractions, such as `Box`, `Rc`, `Arc`, `Vec`,"},{"DocComment":" and `LinkedList`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In rare cases, if your type exposes a way to mutate the value of `T` through a `NonNull<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" and you need to prevent unsoundness from variance (for example, if `T` could be a reference"},{"DocComment":" with a shorter lifetime), you should add a field to make your type invariant, such as"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData<Cell<T>>` or `PhantomData<&'a mut T>`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Example of a type that must be invariant:"},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::Cell;"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" struct Invariant<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     ptr: std::ptr::NonNull<T>,"},{"DocComment":"     _invariant: PhantomData<Cell<T>>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Notice that `NonNull<T>` has a `From` instance for `&T`. However, this does"},{"DocComment":" not change the fact that mutating through a (pointer derived from a) shared"},{"DocComment":" reference is undefined behavior unless the mutation happens inside an"},{"DocComment":" [`UnsafeCell<T>`]. The same goes for creating a mutable reference from a shared"},{"DocComment":" reference. When using this `From` instance without an `UnsafeCell<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" it is your responsibility to ensure that `as_mut` is never called, and `as_ptr`"},{"DocComment":" is never used for mutation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Representation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Thanks to the [null pointer optimization],"},{"DocComment":" `NonNull<T>` and `Option<NonNull<T>>`"},{"DocComment":" are guaranteed to have the same size and alignment:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::ptr::NonNull;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq